Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

All,

Harco mechanical joint restraint is of the full circle style employing mach=
ined serrations.

The geometry of the machined serrations are exactly that of the original de=
sign developed for PVC pipe by Uni-Flange Corporation now owned by Ford Met=
er Box Company and commercialized in the late 1970's.  Patent Number 4,336,=
959 (now expired) was applied for in 1980 and granted in 1982.  All Harco j=
oint restraints utilize this design.  As with the Uni-Flange/Ford Meter Box=
 product the serrations are machined.

For many years the Uni-Flange Series 1300 pipe to pipe restraint was the do=
minant product in the water piping application when joint restraint was emp=
loyed.  Even since the patent expired the Uni-Flange restraint has retained=
 a large share of the restraint market with the same design.  As a result o=
f this conservative approach to design, the successful performance experien=
ce of the Harco design is almost 40 years.  And that experience base line i=
s on a massive scale, in terms of numbers of units in service and variety o=
f applications.

Since the expiration of the patent the other major joint restraint manufact=
urers including EBAA Iron, Star Pipe Products, and Sigma Corporation have i=
ntroduced full circle restraint designs.  All of them utilize machined rest=
raints.  No major manufacturer of joint restraint uses green sand molded as=
 cast gripping features.

Machined serrations of a sharp geometry are required to achieve the grippin=
g power to consistently hold PVC pipe for the thrust forces experienced in =
water piping and to meet the performance requirements of ASTM F1674, "Stand=
ard Test Method for Joint Restraint Products for Use with PVC Pipe".

An as cast gripping feature using a green sand casting method will allow on=
ly "dull and blunt" features and of such variability that gripping is highl=
y variable.  "Every grip ring is different."  In addition, without machinin=
g, it is impossible to hold tight circularity which will further reduce the=
 consistency of gripping.  The upshot of the variability of an as cast grip=
ping surface is one might be too tight or too loose.  In the case of too lo=
ose the joint pulls apart.  And it won't necessarily happen upon immediate =
start up.  To achieve the right and consistent grip it is necessary to mach=
ine the serrations.

The past 40 years of US water infrastructure performance in this regard pro=
ves the efficacy of machined serrations in joint restraints for PVC pipe.  =
"Every grip ring is the same."

[Description: Restraint section 2]


[Description: CLAMP7]



